## Choose the colour mapping

| Relationship | Meaning of colour | Required context |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Categorical | Identifies a different source or device | Stable identifier, readable name and another cue such as a line pattern or marker |
| Sequential | Moves in one ordered direction as a value increases | Measure, unit, minimum, maximum, direction and explicit handling outside the scale |
| Divergent | Shows direction and magnitude away from a meaningful centre | A justified centre, two declared endpoints, signed values and a definition of each direction |
| Missing | There is no usable numeric observation | A label such as “Not supplied”, a non-numeric mark and an explanation where needed |

A categorical colour is not a rank or an amount. Do not use generated device colours as a temperature progression. A divergent centre must mean something, such as no difference from a stated reference. It is not just the midpoint that makes a palette look balanced. A real zero belongs on the numeric scale. An unknown or unavailable reading does not.

Use no more than **two colour scales in one chart** when they are sequential or divergent. This is not a limit of two categorical series. Split the graphic or use a table when more measures make the colour meaning hard to follow.

## Define and test a quantitative scale

State the quantity, units, period, endpoints and any centre before choosing colours. Explain any out-of-range value. Silently giving it the endpoint colour hides information. If the range changes, update the legend and say whether charts from different periods can still be compared.

The example interpolates encoded sRGB channels and rounds each result. Its sequential endpoints are `#D2E2F1` and `#103E65`. Its divergent branches run from `#1D70B8` through the neutral `#F3F3F3` to `#8A6100`. These are fixed, opaque **reference colours**. They are not a second categorical palette, production tokens or a perceptually uniform scale. The in-between hex values are calculated from the documented inputs. A real heatmap or map layer needs its own reviewed range, context and contrast evidence.

Keep labels outside fills unless you have tested the real text and fill pair. Pale scale steps may not contrast with a white canvas. Use visible boundaries and direct labels, or offer a simpler view. Different hex values do not prove that neighbouring areas can be told apart. The worked examples use outlines, written values and matching tables. Where colour contrast is not enough, add shapes, labels or another way to read the data.

A missing value uses a crossed empty swatch and the words “Not supplied”. It has no numeric colour on purpose. With forced colours or fills removed, the labels and data tables still give every value and its missing state.


## Generate from identity

Pass a stable native source or statistic ID to `chartColour(key, {tone, mode})`. The generator calculates an opaque sRGB colour through OKLCH, a colour space that separates lightness, colour strength and hue. It lowers colour strength where needed to fit the screen gamut and adjusts lightness to meet the chart-surface contrast target. Muted means lower colour strength, **not transparency**.

The result is deterministic. The same ID and display settings give the same colour after a reload, a reorder or a legend change. It needs no saved colour map per device and no fixed list of 20 colours. Keep the ID stable when a friendly name changes. Previous-period comparisons and the positive and negative halves of a native power source share their base ID.

Energy roles reserve recognisable colour families: grid import, grid export, solar, gas, water, battery charging, battery discharge and non-fossil energy. Total consumption has a neutral role and a distinct dashed line. Several meters use namespaced keys such as `gas:<statistic ID>`, which keep a family resemblance and add stable variation. Unrelated devices use the wider generated hue range.

The theme also supplies **24 fallback colour variables**, `civica-chart-1` to `civica-chart-24`, for consumers that accept only numbered variables. These are compatibility slots, not a maximum series count. Charts that know the identity use the generator directly. The first eight slots keep their energy-role mapping.

## Vivid, Muted, Light and Dark

The generator still has Vivid and Muted tones. Since 18 November 2026 every civica theme uses Vivid, because the theme colour control has no tone choice.

| Setting | What changes | What stays stable |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Theme colour | Branding and interface accents | Chart identity and energy family |
| Vivid / Muted (generator only) | Chart colour strength and tested lightness | Identity, family and patterns |
| Light / Dark | Contrast against the actual chart surface | Identity, family and patterns |
| Hide, reorder or add a series | Native visibility or ordering | Colours of existing identities |

All chart fills and strokes are opaque. Do not append alpha bytes, use a gradient or lower a series’ opacity to make a second palette. Hover must not thicken a line or fade the other series. Normal, hover and legend lines use the shared **1.5px** adapter constant in every tone and mode, a [documented adaptation](../components/charts.html#visual-rules) of the ONS 3px reference. A previous-period column uses the same identity colour with a hatch pattern, and its original date is in the data table.

## Support colour with another cue

Use `chartSeriesStyle` to get the same colour plus fixed line dashes, markers and fill patterns. The adapter applies the matching keys to native legend items and keeps their visibility controls and accessible labels. Use readable source names, a distinct total line, clear comparison labels and the **View data** table.

Twenty or more generated colours suit large device inventories. They do **not** mean that 20 colours shown together are easy to tell apart, including for people with colour-vision differences. Patterns also have a limited capacity. Prefer fewer visible series, native legend isolation and device detail where available. Never silently delete readings or merge devices to stay under a limit. A 3:1 contrast check against the canvas does not show that every pair of colours is distinct.

The 24-device example shows this limit. Check labels and patterns as well as swatches, and do not approve a palette just because its hex strings differ. When a chart is crowded, keep every value in the table and let native controls focus the chart.

## Maintenance and evidence

The JSON contract records the algorithm version, colour families, contrast surfaces, pattern choices and example IDs. A change to the algorithm may change existing identity colours, so version it and explain it. Run the palette, native-shaped renderer and live-route checks before release.

For the documentation examples, `docs/data/data-colour-examples.yml` records the fixed IDs, original values, generated mappings, generator hash and quantitative formulas under `colour_reference`. Check those records against the visible swatches and tables. Regenerate the categorical references after an algorithm change and do not hand-edit single hex values. These static examples have no theme or device controls and need no JavaScript.

This generated range is a civica extension, not an official ONS or GOV.UK palette. It supports larger Home Assistant inventories while keeping to the advice to limit categories and use colour consistently. The [Styles index](index.html) credits the outside sources.
